## 2. Database Schema (PostgreSQL)

Use SQLAlchemy models + Alembic for migrations. Every table gets `id` (UUID, primary key, `gen_random_uuid()` via `pgcrypto` or `uuid-ossp`), `created_at`, `updated_at` (both `TIMESTAMPTZ`, default `now()`).

### 2.1 `users`
| column | type | constraints |
|---|---|---|
| id | UUID | PK |
| email | VARCHAR(255) | UNIQUE, NOT NULL |
| hashed_password | VARCHAR(255) | NOT NULL |
| full_name | VARCHAR(100) | NOT NULL |
| created_at / updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | default now() |

Index: unique index on `email` (case-insensitive — store lowercased, or use `citext`).

### 2.2 `groups`
| column | type | constraints |
|---|---|---|
| id | UUID | PK |
| name | VARCHAR(100) | NOT NULL |
| created_by | UUID | FK → users.id, NOT NULL |
| invite_code | VARCHAR(10) | UNIQUE, NOT NULL — random alphanumeric, generated on create |
| created_at / updated_at | | |

### 2.3 `group_members` (join table)
| column | type | constraints |
|---|---|---|
| id | UUID | PK |
| group_id | UUID | FK → groups.id, NOT NULL, ON DELETE CASCADE |
| user_id | UUID | FK → users.id, NOT NULL, ON DELETE CASCADE |
| joined_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | default now() |

Constraint: `UNIQUE(group_id, user_id)` — a user can't join the same group twice.

### 2.4 `expenses`
| column | type | constraints |
|---|---|---|
| id | UUID | PK |
| group_id | UUID | FK → groups.id, NOT NULL |
| paid_by | UUID | FK → users.id, NOT NULL — who fronted the money |
| description | VARCHAR(255) | NOT NULL |
| amount | NUMERIC(10,2) | NOT NULL, CHECK (amount > 0) |
| split_type | VARCHAR(20) | NOT NULL, CHECK IN ('equal','percentage','exact') |
| expense_date | DATE | NOT NULL, default current_date |
| version | INTEGER | NOT NULL, default 1 — **optimistic locking column** |
| created_at / updated_at | | |

**Never store `amount_owed` per-person on this table** — that lives in `expense_splits` (below), which is the source of truth for balance calculation.

### 2.5 `expense_splits`
One row per (expense, participant) — how much of that expense each person owes.

| column | type | constraints |
|---|---|---|
| id | UUID | PK |
| expense_id | UUID | FK → expenses.id, NOT NULL, ON DELETE CASCADE |
| user_id | UUID | FK → users.id, NOT NULL |
| amount_owed | NUMERIC(10,2) | NOT NULL, CHECK (amount_owed >= 0) |

Constraint: `UNIQUE(expense_id, user_id)`.
**Invariant you must enforce in application code**: `SUM(amount_owed) for a given expense_id == expenses.amount` exactly (down to the cent). This is where rounding logic lives — see §4.

### 2.6 `settlements`
Records a payment made to clear (part of) a debt between two users in a group.

| column | type | constraints |
|---|---|---|
| id | UUID | PK |
| group_id | UUID | FK → groups.id, NOT NULL |
| paid_by | UUID | FK → users.id, NOT NULL — the debtor, who is paying |
| paid_to | UUID | FK → users.id, NOT NULL — the creditor, receiving |
| amount | NUMERIC(10,2) | NOT NULL, CHECK (amount > 0) |
| settled_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | default now() |

### 2.7 `refresh_tokens`
| column | type | constraints |
|---|---|---|
| id | UUID | PK |
| user_id | UUID | FK → users.id, NOT NULL, ON DELETE CASCADE |
| token_hash | VARCHAR(255) | NOT NULL — store a hash of the refresh token, never plaintext |
| expires_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | NOT NULL |
| revoked | BOOLEAN | default false |
| created_at | | |

Rationale for a DB table instead of pure stateless JWT refresh: lets you revoke on logout and detect reuse (security best practice worth mentioning in interviews).

